# Shape: same-origin. ONE domain serves both apps, path-routed.
# Imported inside the main site block of infra/caddy/Caddyfile when
# MANTLE_CADDY_SHAPE=same-origin (the default). Release-owned; do not edit on
# a box (routes a box needs go in conf.d/).
#
# Server-owned paths: the API plane, the share + print render surfaces, the
# MCP OAuth discovery docs, and every asset a sandboxed frame (a Present
# canvas, a mini app) loads: the runtime bundles and the fonts. Such a frame
# has the opaque origin 'null' and sends no cookies, so the client app would
# answer a bundle with a login redirect and a font without the CORS header a
# font fetch needs; the server serves both CORS-open (server/web/server/
# static.ts). Everything else (owner UI, /login, /hub, /team*, /env.js,
# /_next/*) falls through to the client app. Env for this shape:
# MANTLE_SERVER_ORIGIN=https://<domain> (the client app needs an absolute
# origin for its server-side appearance fetch). MANTLE_CLIENT_SITE_ADDRESS
# and MANTLE_API_CORS_ORIGINS stay unset.
# Calls signalling (calls plan page 886126b4 section 4). The browser
# connects to wss://<site>/livekit (the LiveKit client appends /rtc itself);
# handle_path strips the prefix and Caddy proxies the WebSocket upgrade as
# is. Media never passes through Caddy (the livekit service publishes its
# own ports). With the `calls` profile off the upstream does not resolve
# and the path answers 502, which is right: there is no Call button then.
handle_path /livekit/* {
	reverse_proxy livekit:7880
}
@server path /api /api/* /s /s/* /print /print/* /share-runtime/* /app-runtime/* /canvas-runtime/* /fonts/* /Inter/* /.well-known/oauth-authorization-server /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource
handle @server {
	reverse_proxy web:3000
}
handle {
	reverse_proxy client-web:3000
}
